model.en:
import { Injectable, OnModuleInit } from '@nestjs/common';
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';

@Injectable()
export class PrismaService extends PrismaClient implements OnModuleInit {
  async onModuleInit() {
    await this.$connect();
  }
}

Why: Nest has no first-class Prisma DI, so the PrismaService is the wrapper YOU provide. It must implement OnModuleInit and call await this.$connect() in onModuleInit, the lifecycle hook that runs once the module is initialised — that's where the database connection is opened. Without it the client is injected but never connected, so the first this.prisma.user.* call fails. (Prisma opens lazily on first query in newer versions, but the recipe still wires $connect in onModuleInit so the connection — and any failure — surfaces at startup, not mid-request.)

